---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import PricingTierBuilder from '../../components/PricingTierBuilder.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="SaaS Pricing Tier Builder" description="Design and preview your pricing tiers side by side before publishing them.">
  <PricingTierBuilder client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why seeing tiers side by side matters</h2>
  <p>Pricing tiers are judged relative to each other, not in isolation — a customer decides between tiers by comparing what they get for the price difference, so previewing all tiers together helps you spot whether the value progression actually makes sense before publishing.</p>

  <h2>How to use this builder</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Define each tier's name, price, limits, and key features.</li>
    <li>Review the side-by-side preview.</li>
    <li>Adjust until the value progression between tiers feels clear and justified.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Too many tiers</strong>, creating decision paralysis — most successful pricing pages use 3-4 tiers at most.</li>
    <li><strong>Unclear differentiation between adjacent tiers</strong>, making it hard for a customer to justify upgrading.</li>
    <li><strong>Anchoring the highest tier too low</strong>, capping your potential revenue from customers willing to pay more for more value.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Designing pricing tiers for a new SaaS product launch</li>
    <li>Restructuring existing pricing to improve tier differentiation</li>
    <li>Previewing how tiers will look before implementing them on a real pricing page</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>How many pricing tiers should I offer?</strong> Three tiers is a common sweet spot — enough to serve different customer segments without overwhelming decision-making with too many options.</p>
</ToolLayout>
